# The variables this project reads, with example values, committed so that a
# clone can be turned into a working checkout by copying it:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and this file is not. That split is the point of the
# file and also its hazard: everything written below is published. Name a
# variable here and give its shape -- a URL, an empty credential, a default --
# never a working value. A real key committed once is leaked even after the
# next commit deletes it, because the blob stays in the history.
#
# gitleaks runs at pre-commit and catches the formats it recognizes: an AWS key,
# a GitHub token, a private key block. It does not recognize a password, a
# database URL with credentials in it, or a token this project invented, so a
# leak of one of those reaches the remote with every check green. Read the diff.
#
# Keep this file and the code that reads it in step. A variable the code
# requires and this file omits fails at run time in someone else's checkout,
# with an error naming a key rather than the file it belongs in; a variable
# listed here that nothing reads sends them looking for a value to supply that
# is never consumed. Neither is reported by anything.
#
# The entries below are shapes, not settings this repository has. Replace them
# with the real variables and delete the rest. They ship commented out on
# purpose: an uncommented placeholder copied to `.env` is a configured value
# pointing at nothing, which fails further from the cause than a missing one.

# A service endpoint. Full URL including the scheme.
# API_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com

# A credential. Named here, filled only in `.env` -- this side of the pair is
# the public one.
# API_TOKEN=

# A local-only override. A default that is right on a laptop and wrong anywhere
# shared belongs here rather than hardcoded.
# LOG_LEVEL=debug
